\f1\fs22 Chip Stockwell stood in the middle of his new townhouse, surrounded by boxes he hadn't yet unpacked. The place was immaculate\'97a spacious three-bedroom two-storey townhouse with minimalist decor and high-end finishes that made his previous Fridge Force quarters look like a prison cell. Morning light streamed through floor-to-ceiling windows overlooking Sublime Point's manicured residential circuit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Home sweet home,\'92 he muttered to no one in the kitchen, running his fingers along the quartz countertop.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He tried to avert his gaze from the door to the stasis locker. He\'92d never lived with one before, always preferring an old-fashioned calling fridge. Maybe, he could get this one decommissioned, and turned into \'85 
\f2\i I dunno, a wine cellar
\f1\i0 , he thought. He shook his head; he\'92d never been interested in wine, never finding anything that was compatible with his fast food diet. Mostly because of the glasses, he supposed. He ate most meals straight from the paper bags and wrappers, and had no use for cutlery, so how the tax would he handle a wine class? We was sure he would continually tip it over or splinter its fine stem in his bear-sized hands.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>How would he order fast food now? Would anyone deliver to Eloise\'92s compound?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Within hours of unpacking his essentials, he'd found the screen he\'92d used to divide his one-room living area into dining and TV spaces. His new home had separate TV, dining, and living rooms, so he leant the partially folded screen against the stasis locker door. Out of sight, but never out of mind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Project Kebab facility occupied the lowest sub-level of the building that was the farthest away from everything: the residential circuit, the main house, the administration buildings and even the spectacular views over Jamison Valley. Chip descended in an elevator that required both biometric verification and a keycard. The doors opened out on a modest lounge area, the seating furniture of which Chip could see would lack the sturdiness for his comfort. There was a lonely water cooler against the far wall and Chip wondered what vintage the water in the its large inverted blue bottle would be. There was no evidence of disposable cups. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To the left was a pair of doors marked simply \'93K-LAB.\'94 As Chip approached, the doors slid open. Beyond, he saw an expansive laboratory space with workstations arranged in concentric circles around a central raised glassed-in platform. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The famous Chip Stockwell,\'92 came a voice from his left. \'91I\'92ve heard so little about you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A man in his late thirties with an immaculate suit and perfectly styled hair approached, hand extended. His smile didn't reach his eyes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You must be The Johnny,\'92 Chip replied, accepting the handshake with his own, clammy baseball mitt-sized claw.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Just Johnny will do,\'92 the man said, grip firm and deliberate. \'91Or Mr Stoat. Unless you're one of my life-documenters, in which case I'm your worst nightmare.\'92 He laughed, as if this was a joke they were both in on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m just a stasis engineer,\'92 Chip grumbled, and he did sound something like a bear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s no \'93just\'94 anything at Project Kebab, Stockwell. Everyone here has a purpose. Some of us know what that purpose is.\'92 Johnny stood at an angle, opening up the passage between desks. \'91Come in to my office,\'92 he invited. \'91Unless you would like to see the Kebabs first.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The what?\'92 Chip wondered if The Johnny knew how to smuggle Lebanese food into the facility.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You know,\'92 Johnny whispered. \'91The Gy\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, tax no,\'92 Chip blurted, then covered with. \'91Plenty of time for that later.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In his office, Johnny offered Chip as seat. \'91How much do you know about what we're doing here?\'92 Johnny asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nothing yet,\'92 Chip confessed, and sensed that he would prefer to keep it that way.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny's smile widened fractionally. \'91We\'92re researching applications for the Kebabs\'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip eyes went as wide as teacup saucers. \'91What applications?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny chuckled. \'91Well you must admit, your Fridge Force has been keeping a great secret from the wider scientific community for decades. Applying these things to the most mediocre of uses. What? Keeping milk from going sour?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip was so silent, Johnny kept blathering. \'91I mean, did you guys even consider the possibilities for energy generation? I mean, these Kebabs,\'92\'97again with the air quotes\'97\'91could be the greatest thing since nuclear.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip gagged. \'91Excuse me,\'92 he spluttered. \'91I think I ate some bad\'85\'92 He hefted his great weight off the chair and lumbered out of the room.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny knew Chip wasn\'92t up for this project. After Chip returned to his office with a little more colour in his face, Johnny led Chip to a another office off the main laboratory.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sarah,\'92 Johnny announced, \'91Your new pet has arrived.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked up, fixing Chip with an analytical gaze that made him feel like a specimen under a microscope. \'91Ignore The Johnny, Mr Stockwell. I\'92m Sarah Jacobson.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip grunted. Sarah gestured him to a chair. As he took seat, Chip noticed three elderly individuals sitting silently in the corner of the room. Their presence was so unobtrusive that he'd completely missed them upon entering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Let me introduce the Sages,\'92 Sarah said, following his gaze. \'91They provide... alternative perspectives on our work.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The three nodded in unison, but didn't speak.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Alternative perspectives?\'92 Chip asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Let's just say they approach Kebab tech  from a more philosophical angle. Some might call it mystical, but I prefer to think of it as intuitive science.\'92 Sarah pulled up a holographic display. \'91Let me show you what we're dealing with.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The display showed a Gyges Disc from multiple angles, along with cascading data streams that Chip couldn't fully parse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The Kebabs exhibit properties that defy our current understanding of physics,\'92 Sarah explained. \'91They create localised chronological variances\'97essentially micro-pockets of altered time.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fascinating,\'92 Chip uttered without commitment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is it fascinating?\'92 Sarah asked. \'92To you, Chip, is it fascinating.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip shrugged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s how stasis works, isn\'92t it? It literally freezes things, not by temperature but in time. Kebab tech preserves things by keeping them in their own bubble in time. In stasis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That's impossible,\'92 Chip said automatically.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I don\'92t think it is impossible,\'92 The Johnny stated flatly. \'91And neither does anyone else in this room.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Shilo sat forward between the other Sages and said, \'91I\'92m twenty-six.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip regarded the wizened old husk of a man and said, \'92That\'92s nice. I hoped you lived a full life.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> \'91Twenty-six. Hundred. Years. Old,\'92 Shilo schooled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip blinked away the absurdity of  Shilo\'92s claim, and cast his eye over the others in the room. He said to The Johnny, \'91I don\'92t care what Goldilocks and the three fossils think. It\'92s impossible.\'92 He heaved his weight off the chair. \'91This was a mistake. I\'92m leaving.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You can\'92t leave,\'92 Sarah stated flatly. \'91You know this is too important to leave in anyone else\'92s hands. We need you. We need you asses the risk of our research and tell us when we\'92re going too far.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Still standing and trying to ignore the complaints of his feet, Chip answered, \'91That\'92s easy. You\'92re already going too far.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92That cannot be true, young Chip,\'92 Kleoboulos stated gently. \'91Your own Fridge Force has been harnessing and monitoring these instruments for many a long year.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And we\'92re all  terrified of when something catastrophic will happen with them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Which is why we need you,\'92 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the time Saturday arrived, Chip had settled into a routine of sorts. Mornings and afternoons were spent at the lab, evenings were devoted to unpacking and setting up his townhouse, and nights were plagued by dreams of Fridge Force agents breaking down his door. The most frightening part of those nightmares were that none of the arresting officers seemed to know his name.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He'd installed three additional locks and a private security system that wasn't connected to Sublime Point's network which, unbeknown to him, Eloise had already neutralised. She also knew that the generous signing bonus she\'92d given Chip had already been split between multiple anonymous accounts, ready for Chip\'92s quick escape if necessary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Contrary to his fears, this job stressed him no more than his Fridge Force gig. While expanding stasis tech beyond its currently mild applications, he realised he\'92d always lived with the expectation that sooner or later, it would blow up in his face. Now that he had a clearer idea of how that might actually happen, he was somehow more at peace with it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Still, if there was one thing he was more stressed about, it was the family living in the townhouse next door. The Chuglinghams, the family he had not long ago been instrumental in sending offshore detention. He felt no guilt for it, only mounting frustration at how happy they were. While they had been avoiding him assiduously, it was evident by the sounds of activities that came from their yard and through their open windows, they were getting on with their lives while he, essentially, didn\'92t have one. They were happy and chirpy and it annoyed the tax out of him. The kids were worse. They were chirpy and loud. Chip had no idea that laughter could sound so threatening.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While he breakfasted on a burrito (Eloise had personally hired a Fast Food Delivery Consultant for him), Chip checked his security system (which he still believed was functioning) on his encrypted tablet (which Eloise had easily jail-breaked). On the dark web portal he'd set up for monitoring Fridge Force communications he found what he feared\'97an internal bulletin listing him as a "person of interest" in an investigation of technological espionage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His tablet pinged with a message from an unknown sender:\
